---
.title = "Object-capability RPC for OCapN, in Zig",
.description = "pancake is a Zig implementation of OCapN and CapTP: two programs pass each other object references and call methods across the network, with no ambient authority to fall back on.",
.date = @date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "index.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Two programs pass each other references to objects and call methods across the
network, under the same rule as local code. A peer can call only the references
it has been handed, and it cannot construct one it was not given, so there is
no ambient authority to fall back on. That is
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn). pancake is a
[Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library that speaks it.

Most network authorization works the other way around. You prove who you are,
the service looks up what you are allowed to do, and anything you hand your
credentials to inherits all of it. With references there is nothing to inherit:
a program gets the objects you passed it and has no way to name anything else.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) holds that against the protocols you already
run.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is the whole permission to call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

```zig
// Both budgets default to 0, which means "wait forever"; a client always
// passes finite ones.
const opts = .{ .handshake_timeout_ms = 10_000, .settle_timeout_ms = 10_000 };
const session = try pancake.driver.connect(allocator, &.{tcp_resident.netlayer()}, partner, .ephemeral, opts);
const greeter = try session.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
const settled = try session.call(greeter, .{
    .verb = "greet",
    .args = &.{.{ .string = "world" }},
}, arena.allocator());
```

That is the conversation: dial, fetch a capability by name, call it. The
allocators and the transport around it are a few more lines.
[Start building]($link.page('start')) walks all of them in Zig, then points at
the same entry in C and each binding.

The protocol core does no I/O of its own. It is a synchronous state machine you
feed bytes into and drain bytes out of, so sockets, threads, and scheduling stay
in your program where you can see them.

Peers can meet without an application server brokering them: the
[iroh transport]($link.page('transports/iroh')) carries CapTP, the call
protocol inside OCapN, over encrypted QUIC
and names each peer by a self-authenticating key. It is an optional artifact: a
TCP-only program never pays for it. Two browsers can meet the same way over a
direct [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) DataChannel, with no server on
the byte path once the channel is up. The two are not equally proven: iroh is
checked on every push and WebRTC is checked only when someone runs it, which
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) sets out row by row.

Hold the library as a Zig package, a plain C ABI, or a native binding (Rust,
TypeScript, Python, Racket). The TypeScript binding also compiles to WebAssembly,
so the same session runs in the browser. A
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) sits beside those surfaces: `zig build` puts the
`pancake` binary in `zig-out/bin`.

This site is the front door. The deeper documents, the ones kept in step with
today's code, live
[next to the source](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs).
New to the ideas? [Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) starts from the protocols you
already run, then [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) goes a level down. Ready
to dial? [Start building]($link.page('start')).
